From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 15:13:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a DRM window can be the size of its screen

A desktop on a tty came up black with nothing wrong in any log, and this is
why.

`ScreenManager::UpdateControllerToWindowMapping` binds a window to a display
controller through `FindWindowAt`, which compares the window's rectangle to
`gfx::Rect(controller->origin(), controller->GetModeSize())` for EXACT
equality. A window that is not precisely the CRTC's rect gets no controller,
and every frame it submits is dropped before it reaches the kernel.

Chromium's default window is `kWindowMaxDefaultWidth` wide and the work area
inset by `kWindowTilePixels` -- 1050x1900 at (10, 10) on a 2880x1920 panel --
so out of the box nothing is ever scanned out. Nothing on a tty maximises a
window either: there is no window manager and no session to restore bounds
from.

TWO THINGS WERE MISSING, and either alone is still a black screen.

`DrmWindowHost::SetFullscreen` is an empty body upstream, on the premise behind
the seven NOTREACHED()s patch 0015 answered: ash sizes its own root window to
the display, so nothing on ChromeOS asks a DRM window to go fullscreen. It is
implemented here, along with `GetPlatformWindowState`, which upstream answers
`kUnknown` forever -- making `DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::IsFullscreen()`
permanently false, so views re-enters fullscreen on every request and the
`DCHECK_EQ` in its own `SetFullscreen` fails in a DCHECK build.

AND NOTHING WAS CALLING IT. `--start-fullscreen` is honoured in
`StartupBrowserCreatorImpl::MaybeToggleFullscreen`, which an --app= launch
never reaches: `MaybeLaunchAppShortcutWindow` answers the startup sequence
with `return true` before `LaunchBrowserForLastProfiles` is ever called. So
the flag is silently dropped for exactly the launch Domicile uses, and the
window implementation above would have sat there unreachable. It is honoured
in that path now.

Kiosk is deliberately not mirrored: it carries a policy surface -- accelerator
stripping, forced app mode -- that this fork has no business turning on as a
side effect of drawing a desktop.

The bounds come from `display::Screen`, and that it agrees with the CRTC is by
construction rather than luck: `DrmScreen` builds its display list from the
same `DisplaySnapshot`s the modeset driver configures from, so a display's
bounds and `gfx::Rect(controller->origin(), GetModeSize())` are one rectangle
-- including after a mode change or a hotplug. Which is why this is fullscreen
rather than a size on the command line: the panel this was found on advertises
more than one preferred mode, so any number written down is a guess.

The decision itself is a free function with four tests in
`domicile/drm_fullscreen.cc`, for the reason `DrmModeset`'s arithmetic is one:
what is left in the host is a state toggle over platform singletons it is
handed rather than owns.
